you all seem too young to have known  Gilda personally I'm just gonna make  that leap so where did your lives  intersect at what point and basically  why Gilda like what was it that made you  three wanted to make her a part of your  life and fight for her well about seven  years ago I started doing fundraising  videos for Gilda's Club in New York City  so Gilda's Club is a place for people  with cancer that was founded by Gene  Wilder and killed his friends after um  passed away and I just fell in love with  Gilda's Club itself and you walk into  Gilda's Club and those murals of Gilda  so I started to read her book and when I  would interview the members for these  fundraising videos they would talk about  Gilda as if she was a friend  they really identified with what she was  going through and I just thought her  legacy was really unique cuz her place  in comedy is is pretty solid but she  also has a really amazing legacy in a  world of cancer where she really helps  many many people so my family is  actually close friends with the Radner  family and has been since long before I  was born so I knew Gilda when I was a  kid and have no memories of her and she  sent presents and was just the loveliest  friend and her brother Michael is my  godfather so of course when Lisa started  making the film he reached out to me and  let me know that she was making it and  when we finally had a chance to meet it  I saw the assembly cut of the film and  just was so relieved at what a beautiful  and brilliant job Lisa had done and just  as someone who you know coming from the  front perspective I was just really  amazed and just and relieved just that  she had done such a great job and with  such sensitive topics of the ups and  downs of her life so I knew I wanted to  help her in whatever way she she wanted  so and James I was lucky enough to see  Gilda on Broadway as a young kid my  brother's took me to see her and I  became instantly I felt instantly in  love with her her spirit her childlike  qualities  and to just to see her create all of  those characters these beautifully  imperfect human beings I related to  Emily Litella on some level and Judy  Miller and and and certainly the nerd  lisa loopner like i wanted to be loved  and i wanted to be heard so i was a  fanboy from from way back when  and got it got to go back backstage  snapped the picture with her or four of  her and got her autograph and that was  it I couldn't talk to her I see that she  was larger than life and my I can hear  my brothers in the background I gave me  on say something but that wasn't gonna  happen and and then it changed my life  it changed my professional life didn't  know it at the time but I'd be connected  to her and various projects I when I  started watching it I didn't know that  Gilda would be talking to me and I  literally just went it took my breath  away and I was like I was startled and  then immediately comforted and just it  was just like this wonderful connection  and I thought okay the first question  I'm gonna ask is how did those audio  files come to you what are they really  cuz is it like this you know the  Watergate tapes are these the little  cassette tapes that you you got a stash  of and you found and was that early on  or did that happen later in the process  or well what you're looking at is about  18 different sources of tapes okay but  the way it started was about halfway  through the project I was sort of  shooting and raising money and shooting  and taking time off and Gilda's brother  gave me access to her boxes that had  been in storage and in those boxes were  and they were like 35 boxes it wasn't it  was like his stuff his mother's stuff  Gilda stuff and in it  there was a tape that I was looking for  cuz in Gilda's book she talked about  some of the things she had filmed and  she filmed her ninth chemotherapy video  and that's what I really wanted to find  so the first trip to Detroit we were  able to find that tape and you see that  in the film okay  so then with that it was really  overwhelming how much stuff was there  because they were just overwhelming but  I have remembered seeing a box of tapes  in there and in Gilda's book she talked  about making the ninth chemotherapy but  in her audio but she did an audiobook of  her book and that wasn't in there so I  said to Michael there was a box of tapes  can you send me the box of tapes and I  thought they were the the Masters for  the audiobook but they were actually the  the way Gilda wrote her book it's always  something is she would record her life  story to both her audio her cassette  player and she would sit with somebody  and just talk over a period of months so  those I had so I had 32 hours of audio  tape of Gilda telling her story Wow but  they were extremely damaged so the audio  was really really bad so the first  rendition of the film was the first  assembly of the film was using all that  bad audio mm-hmm so it was a very it was  a similar film but it was just with a  really bad audio and my filmmaker  friends were saying well it's okay  because it's gold of telling her story  and my regular friends were like you  can't have a film like this because no  one wants to read subtitles well they're  watching a film so then we all went on a  mission to find interviews that she had  done that we can't Meryl found from her  her University of Michigan you get to  tell stories yeah they were at the  Michigan daily and just happened to  interview her and so Lisa was like well  that's if they have audio too because  they had pictures and they're like  actually yeah we recorded our interview  with her and we have the audio tape it  was it was two college students who came  to SNL and they they interviewed her and  they kept the tape all forty-something  years later so some of the that is their  tape and then there's some other sources  like that so but that was really the  goal was to have Gil to tell her story  you folded in of course not only the  recollections of her colleagues and the  people who knew her in her lifetime  but then this one  full lens of what we would call you know  the comics of today you know and the  people that maybe didn't know her or  knew her as a child but are influenced  by her the the modern-day comics that  you have reading her journal and talking  about her so was that part of the  initial spine of the story that you  developed did you always sort of want to  have like the historical recollections  mirroring against the current day  influences I to me the film was always  always about legacy so I thought that  would be really important to show that  and to show guiltless place in comedy  whereas Gilda was influenced by Lucille  Ball and Charlie Chaplin and all these  people she goes on to influence Amy and  Tina Fey and most McCarthy who are  influencing now the new generation you  know new so I wanted to show her place  yeah it was important and I liked that  it wasn't just gender specific that you  know bill was there too and well that  thanks to James because I was the guy it  has to be feel hate her so much  childlike quality in his work truly  maybe not him Barry so much but if you  look at the SNL clips it's he's he's got  that little little boy in him what would  you say that she has taught you  personally what's your takeaway I say  keep the child in you alive always there  was always a big part of all of her  characters so I try to act like a big  kid all the time till the bitter end  yeah she did that I would say just her  bravery when faced with cancer and  life's challenges and how humor got her  through it and it's you seeing it on  screen was is really amazing as I've  heard stories of how brave she was but I  just I can't I rarely have seen I mean  ever anyone so brave with cancer and to  do it on screen and it's just  inspirational to me for you know and I  think other people are getting that too  so yeah I would say similar finding what  I was always impressed by Gilda is that  she could find the humor in the darkest  of times and  I wish I could do that you know you  that's like it's really hard I know I  try it's really hard and also she never  apparently she never forgot anybody's  birthday oh that's a gift yeah that's a  good size started putting in the  calendar like you know every year so  that I will start to remember birthday  so that's it yeah  thank you all very much thank you so  much

